      </div>
      <p class="wl-fine">Multisig adds a second backup obligation. As well as each seed, you must keep the wallet descriptor, the record of which public keys make up the wallet and how many signatures it needs, because the seeds alone may not be enough to rebuild it. Store the descriptor with your backups; it cannot spend anything on its own.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="wl-about doc-prose">
      <p>A bitcoin wallet does not hold coins. It holds the keys that authorize spending the coins recorded on the chain, which is why the same balance can appear in any wallet restored from the same seed, and why the seed is the thing worth protecting; the <a href="/learn/self-custody">self-custody guide</a> covers that in full. Software chosen from this page should be verified before it runs, using the <a href="/verify">signature walkthrough</a>, and hardware should be bought from the manufacturer, with the ordering-privacy habits above. If you are still deciding where the coins come from, see <a href="/buy">buying bitcoin</a> and <a href="/learn/no-kyc">no-KYC bitcoin</a>; if they are for spending, <a href="/spend/gift-cards">gift cards</a> is one route. Affiliate relationships on this page, once any exist, are labeled where they appear and documented in the <a href="/disclosure">disclosure</a>. Everything here is informational, not advice, per the <a href="/terms">terms</a>.</p>
